Q1) What is the difference between stack and heap memory?
-> Stack memory is automatically managed and mainly used for local variables and function calls. It is very fast but limited in size. Heap memory is dynamically allocated using new or malloc and must be manually deallocated using delete or free. Heap is flexible and suitable for runtime memory allocation, but improper handling can cause memory leaks and dangling pointers.

Q2) What is shallow copy and deep copy?
->Shallow copy copies pointer addresses, so multiple objects share same memory location. This can cause issues like double deletion and data corruption. Deep copy allocates separate memory and copies actual data, making objects independent and safe. In real-time projects involving dynamic memory handling, we generally implement deep copy using a custom copy constructor and assignment operator.

Q3) What is copy constructor?
->A copy constructor is a special constructor in C++ used to create a new object as a copy of an existing object. It is called when an object is initialized using another object of the same class. By default, the compiler provides a copy constructor, but for classes using dynamic memory, we usually implement our own copy constructor to perform deep copy and avoid shallow copy issues.


Q. virtul function-
A virtual function is a member function in a base class that you declare using the virtual keyword. It allows the derived class to provide its own implementation, and the correct function is called at runtime based on the actual object type, not the pointer type.

 e.g. without virtual function
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal
{
public:
    void speak()
    {
        cout << "Animal speaks" << endl;
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    void speak()
    {
        cout << "Dog barks" << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Animal *ptr = new Dog();

    ptr->speak();

    delete ptr;
    return 0;
}
Output
Animal speaks
Why?

Although ptr points to a Dog, its type is Animal*. Since speak() is not virtual, C++ calls the base class version.

Why do we need a virtual function?

Without virtual, C++ decides which function to call based on the pointer type (compile-time).

With virtual, C++ decides based on the actual object type (runtime).

e.g. with virtual fun
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal
{
public:
    virtual void speak()
    {
        cout << "Animal speaks" << endl;
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    void speak() override
    {
        cout << "Dog barks" << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Animal *ptr = new Dog();

    ptr->speak();

    delete ptr;
    return 0;
}
Output
Dog barks

Now C++ checks the actual object (Dog) at runtime and calls Dog::speak().


Q4) Why do we use virtual functions?
->Virtual functions are used to achieve runtime polymorphism in C++. They allow us to call the derived class function using a base class pointer or reference. This helps in writing flexible, extensible, and loosely coupled code, especially in large-scale applications and framework-based designs.

*Pure virtual functions:-
-> A pure virtual function is a virtual function declared with = 0. It has no implementation in the base class and forces every derived class to provide its own implementation. A class containing at least one pure virtual function becomes an abstract class, meaning its objects cannot be created. Pure virtual functions are used to define a common interface and achieve runtime polymorphism.

Virtual Function-
Base class says: "I already have an implementation. You may override it if you want."

Pure Virtual Function- virtual void display() = 0;
Base class says: "I don't have an implementation for this behavior. You must implement it in the derived class."

Q. When do you use a virtual function and when do you use a pure virtual function?
answer:
Virtual function: I use it when I need runtime polymorphism. The base class provides a default implementation, and derived classes can override it if needed. It is typically used with inheritance and base class pointers or references.

Pure virtual function: I use it when I want to create an abstract class and force every derived class to provide its own implementation. This is useful when the base class defines only a common interface and cannot provide meaningful behavior itself.

Q5) Difference between compile-time and runtime polymorphism?
-> Compile-time polymorphism is achieved using function or operator overloading, where the compiler resolves the function call during compilation. Runtime polymorphism is achieved using virtual functions and inheritance, where the correct overridden function is selected at runtime using base class pointers or references. Compile-time polymorphism is faster, while runtime polymorphism is more flexible and commonly used in scalable real-world applications.

Q6) What is function overloading vs overriding?
-> Function overloading means multiple functions with the same name but different parameters within the same class. It is an example of compile-time polymorphism. Function overriding means redefining a base class virtual function in the derived class with the same signature. It is an example of runtime polymorphism.

Q7) What is constructor overloading?
-> Constructor overloading means defining multiple constructors in the same class with different parameter lists. It allows objects to be initialized in different ways depending on the requirement. It is an example of compile-time polymorphism.

Q8) What is dynamic memory allocation?
-> Dynamic memory allocation is the process of allocating memory during runtime using heap memory. In C++, we use new and delete operators for this purpose. It is useful when the memory requirement is not fixed at compile time, such as in dynamic data structures, file handling, networking, and large-scale applications. Proper memory deallocation is important to avoid memory leaks and dangling pointers.

Q9) Difference between new/delete and malloc/free?
-> new and delete are C++ operators used for dynamic memory allocation and deallocation, while malloc and free are C library functions. The major difference is that new calls constructors and delete calls destructors, whereas malloc and free only allocate and release raw memory without object initialization or cleanup.

Q10) What is a memory leak?
-> A memory leak occurs when dynamically allocated memory is not released after use. As a result, memory remains occupied even though the program no longer needs it. Over time, memory leaks can increase memory consumption, reduce application performance, and may eventually crash the system.

Q11) What is dangling pointer?
-> A dangling pointer is a pointer that refers to memory that has already been freed or gone out of scope. Accessing such pointers causes undefined behavior, crashes, or garbage values. Dangling pointers commonly occur after using delete, returning addresses of local variables, or accessing objects after scope completion. To avoid them, we should reset pointers to nullptr and prefer smart pointers in modern C++ applications.
e.g.
    int main()
    {
    int *p = new int(10);
    delete p;
    cout << *p; // here will see garbage value to avoid this, we will use p = nullptr or smart ptr
    return 0;
    }

Q12) What is smart pointer?
-> Smart pointers are C++ library classes that automatically manage dynamic memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers. They automatically release memory when the object is no longer needed. Smart pointers are part of modern C++ and follow the RAII principle.
